 "capsule": "The window 592-922 covers the early medieval Pandya revival centered on Madurai, beginning with Kadungon's overthrow of Kalabhra rule around 590 and running through the dynasty's long rivalry with the Pallavas and later the Cholas for control of the Tamil country. Rulers such as Maravarman Rajasimha I (c. 730-765), who broke Pallava power along the Kaveri, his son Jatila Parantaka Nedunjadaiyan (r. 765-815), who defeated Nandivarman II Pallavamalla at Pennagadam around 767, and Srimara Srivallabha (r. 815-862), who raided Sri Lanka and briefly held Anuradhapura, mark its high points. The line was eclipsed after Parantaka Chola I's decisive defeat of the Pandyas around 916-922, reducing them to Chola vassalage for nearly three centuries.",
